</p>

<p>
	Within a few years there would be more than fifty companies in the area making
	semiconductors. The <strong>exponential growth</strong> of this industry was
	correlated with the phenomenon famously discovered by Moore, who in 1965 drew
	a graph of the speed of integrated circuits, <strong>based on</strong> the
	number of transistors that could be placed on a chip, and showed that it
	doubled about every two years, a <strong>trajectory</strong> that could be
	expected to continue. This was <strong>reaffirmed</strong> in 1971, when Intel
	was able to <strong>etch</strong> a complete central processing unit onto one
	chip, the Intel 4004, which was <strong>dubbed</strong> a “microprocessor.”
</p>
