<p class="translation">
	Когда в мае 1980 года Apple III начал продаваться, он провалился. Рэнди
	Виггинтон, один из инженеров, подытожил: «Apple III был как ребёнок, зачатый
	на групповой оргии: потом у всех болела голова, получился внебрачный ребёнок,
	и каждый говорил: „Это не мой“».
</p>

<p>
	By then Jobs had <strong>distanced himself from</strong> the Apple III and was
	<strong>thrashing about</strong> for ways to produce something more radically
	different. At first he <strong>flirted with</strong> the idea of touchscreens,
	but he found himself frustrated. At one demonstration of the technology, he
	arrived late, <strong>fidgeted</strong> awhile, then abruptly
	<strong>cut off</strong> the engineers in the middle of their presentation
	with a <strong>brusque</strong> “Thank you.” They were confused. “Would you
	like us to leave?” one asked. Jobs said yes, then <strong>berated</strong> his
	colleagues for wasting his time.
</p>
