	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td><strong>prove me wrong</strong></td>
		<td>доказать, что я неправ</td>
	</tr>
</table>
<p class="translation">
	Затем он создал для Apple II версию языка Pascal — языка программирования
	высокого уровня. Джобс противился, считая, что Apple II хватит BASIC, но
	сказал Аткинсону: «Раз ты так этим горишь, даю тебе шесть дней, чтобы
	доказать, что я неправ». Тот доказал, и с тех пор Джобс его уважал.
</p>

<p>
	By the fall of 1979 Apple was <strong>breeding three ponies</strong> to be
	potential successors to the Apple II <strong>workhorse</strong>. There was the
	<strong>ill-fated</strong> Apple III. There was the Lisa project, which was
	beginning to disappoint Jobs. And somewhere
	<strong>off Jobs’s radar screen</strong>, at least for the moment, there was a
	small <strong>skunkworks</strong> project for a low-cost machine that was
	being developed by a colorful employee named Jef Raskin, a former professor
	who had taught Bill Atkinson. Raskin’s goal was to make an inexpensive
	“computer for the masses” that would be like an <strong>appliance</strong>—a
	self-contained unit with computer, keyboard, monitor, and software all
	together—and have a graphical interface. He tried to
	<strong>turn his colleagues on to</strong> a
	<strong>cutting-edge</strong> research center, right in Palo Alto, that was
	pioneering such ideas.
</p>
