	Фаделл, ошарашенный процессом: «Я привык к Philips, где такие решения занимали
	совещание за совещанием, с кучей презентаций и уходом на доработку».
</p>

<p>
	Next it was Phil Schiller’s turn. “Can I bring out my idea now?” he asked. He
	left the room and returned with a handful of iPod models, all of which had the
	same device on the front: the soon-to-be-famous <strong>trackwheel</strong>.
	“I had been thinking of how you go through a playlist,” he recalled. “You
	can’t press a button hundreds of times. Wouldn’t it be great if you could have
	a wheel?” By turning the wheel with your thumb, you could
	<strong>scroll through songs</strong>. The longer you kept turning, the faster
	the scrolling got, so you could <strong>zip through</strong>
	hundreds easily. Jobs shouted, “That’s it!” He got Fadell and the engineers
	working on it.
</p>
