</p>

<p>
	In order to make the iPod really easy to use—and this
	<strong>took a lot of arguing</strong> on my part—we needed to limit what the
	device itself would do. Instead we put that functionality in iTunes on the
	computer. For example, we made it so you couldn’t make playlists using the
	device. You made playlists on iTunes, and then you
	<strong>synced with</strong> the device. That was
	<strong>controversial</strong>. But what made the Rio and other devices so
	<strong>brain-dead</strong> was that they were complicated. They had to do
	things like make playlists, because they weren’t integrated with the jukebox
	software on your computer. So by owning the iTunes software and the iPod
	device, that allowed us to make the computer and the device work together, and
	it allowed us to put the complexity in the right place.
</p>
