<p>
	His struggle with Apple Corps, the Beatles’ business holding company,
	stretched more than three decades, causing too many journalists to use the
	phrase “long and winding road” in stories about the relationship. It began in
	1978, when Apple Computers, soon after its launch, was sued by Apple Corps for
	<strong>trademark infringement</strong>, based on the fact that the Beatles’ former recording
	label was called Apple. The suit was settled three years later, when Apple
	Computers paid Apple Corps $80,000. The settlement had what seemed back then
	<strong>an innocuous stipulation</strong>: The Beatles would not produce any computer equipment
	and Apple would not market any music products.
</p>
