	</tr>
</table>

<p class="translation">
	Реальной власти совету Джобс не уступал, но его заседания использовал, чтобы
	доверительно обкатывать идеи и продумывать стратегии, — стоял у доски и вёл
	свободные дискуссии. Восемнадцать месяцев директора обсуждали переход на
	архитектуру Intel. «Мы спорили, задавали много вопросов и наконец все решили:
	это нужно сделать», — вспоминал член совета Арт Левинсон.
</p>

<p>
	Paul Otellini, who was then president and later became CEO of Intel, began
	<strong>huddling with</strong> Jobs. They had gotten to know each other when Jobs was
	struggling to keep NeXT alive and, as Otellini later put it, “his arrogance
	had been temporarily <strong>tempered</strong>.” Otellini has a calm and <strong>wry</strong> take on people,
	and he was amused rather than put off when he discovered, upon dealing with
	Jobs at Apple in the early 2000s, “that <strong>his juices were going again</strong>, and he
	wasn’t nearly as humble anymore.” Intel had deals with other computer makers,
	and Jobs wanted a better price than they had. “We had to find creative ways to
	<strong>bridge the numbers</strong>,” said Otellini. Most of the negotiating was done, as Jobs
	preferred, on long walks, sometimes on the trails up to the radio telescope
	known as the Dish above the Stanford campus. Jobs would start the walk by
	telling a story and explaining how he saw the history of computers evolving.
	By the end he would be <strong>haggling over price</strong>.
</p>
