		<td>уничтожался</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td><strong>render the iPod unnecessary</strong></td>
		<td>сделать iPod ненужным</td>
	</tr>
</table>

<p class="translation">
	Поэтому Джобс тревожился. «Он постоянно думал, что может нам навредить», —
	вспоминал член совета Арт Левинсон. Вывод, к которому он пришёл: «Устройство,
	способное отобрать наш обед, — мобильный телефон». Он объяснял совету: рынок
	цифровых камер уничтожается телефонами с камерами. То же может случиться с
	iPod, если производители телефонов начнут встраивать в них плееры. «Телефон
	есть у каждого — iPod может стать ненужным».
</p>

<p>
	His first strategy was to do something that he had admitted in front of Bill
	Gates was not in his DNA: to partner with another company. He began talking to
	Ed Zander, the new CEO of Motorola, about making <strong>a companion to</strong> Motorola’s
	popular RAZR, which was a cell phone and digital camera, that would have an
	iPod built in. Thus was born the ROKR. It ended up having neither the enticing
	minimalism of an iPod nor the convenient slimness of a RAZR. Ugly, difficult
	to load, and with <strong>an arbitrary hundred-song limit</strong>, it had all <strong>the hallmarks of</strong>
	a product that had been <strong>negotiated by a committee</strong>, which was counter to the
	way Jobs liked to work. Instead of hardware, software, and content all being
	controlled by one company, they were <strong>cobbled together</strong> by Motorola, Apple, and
	the wireless carrier Cingular. “You call this the phone of the future?”
	<strong>Wired</strong> <strong>scoffed</strong> on its November 2005 cover.
</p>
