<p class="translation">
	Следующим было стекло. «Покончив с металлом, я посмотрел на Джони и сказал:
	надо овладеть стеклом», — говорил Джобс. Для Apple Store уже создали огромные
	витрины и стеклянные лестницы. Для iPhone по плану экрану полагался пластик,
	как у iPod. Но Джобс решил: стекло будет выглядеть куда элегантнее и
	основательнее. И взялся искать прочное, устойчивое к царапинам стекло.
</p>

<p>
	The natural place to look was Asia, where the glass for the stores was being
	made. But Jobs’s friend John Seeley Brown, who was on the board of Corning
	Glass in Upstate New York, told him that he should talk to that company’s
	young and dynamic CEO, Wendell Weeks. So he dialed the main Corning
	<strong>switchboard</strong> number and asked to be <strong>put through</strong> to Weeks. He got an assistant,
	who offered to pass along the message. “No, I’m Steve Jobs,” he replied. “Put
	me through.” The assistant refused. Jobs called Brown and complained that he
	had been subjected to “typical <strong>East Coast bullshit</strong>.” When Weeks heard that, he
	called the main Apple <strong>switchboard</strong> and asked to speak to Jobs. He was told to
	put his request in writing and send it in by fax. When Jobs was told what
	happened, he <strong>took a liking to</strong> Weeks and invited him to Cupertino.
</p>
