	Amazon’s Jeff Bezos had tried to take a similar approach with ebooks,
	insisting on selling them for <strong>at most</strong> $9.99. Jobs came in and <strong>offered
	publishers</strong> what he had refused to offer record companies: They could <strong>set any
	price</strong> they wanted for their <strong>wares</strong> in the iBooks Store, and Apple would <strong>take
	30%</strong>. Initially that meant prices were higher than on Amazon. Why would people
	pay Apple more? “That won’t be the case,” Jobs answered, when Walt Mossberg
	asked him that question at the iPad launch event. “The price will be the
	same.” He was right.
</p>

