	дизайн. Неудивительно, что Джобсу он не понравился. «Не позволите ли нашим
	дизайнерам помочь?» — спросил он. Мёрдок согласился. «Дизайнеры Apple
	попробовали, — вспоминал Мёрдок, — наши ребята вернулись и попробовали ещё
	раз, через десять дней мы показали оба варианта — и ему на самом деле больше
	понравился вариант нашей команды. Мы были ошарашены».
</p>

<p>
	<strong>The Daily</strong>, which was neither <strong>tabloidy</strong> nor serious, but
	instead a rather <strong>midmarket</strong> product like <strong>USA Today</strong>, was not
	very successful. But it did help create an <strong>odd-couple bonding</strong> between Jobs and
	Murdoch. When Murdoch asked him to speak at his June 2010 News Corp. annual
	<strong>management retreat</strong>, Jobs <strong>made an exception</strong> to his rule of never doing such
	appearances. James Murdoch led him in an after-dinner interview that lasted
	almost two hours. “He was very <strong>blunt</strong> and <strong>critical</strong> of what newspapers were
	doing in technology,” Murdoch recalled. “He told us we were going to find it
	hard to get things right, because you’re in New York, and anyone who’s any
	good at tech works in Silicon Valley.” This did not <strong>go down very well</strong> with the
	president of the Wall Street Journal Digital Network, Gordon McLeod, who
	<strong>pushed back a bit</strong>. At the end, McLeod came up to Jobs and said, “Thanks, it
	was a wonderful evening, but you probably just <strong>cost me my job</strong>.” Murdoch
	<strong>chuckled</strong> a bit when he described the scene to me. “It ended up being true,” he
	said. McLeod was <strong>out within three months</strong>.
</p>

