<h1>CHAPTER THIRTY-NINE</h1>
<h2><strong>NEW BATTLES</strong></h2>
<h2>And Echoes of Old Ones</h2>

<p><strong>Google: Open versus Closed</strong></p>
<p class="translation">Google: открытость против закрытости</p>

<p>
	A few days after he <strong>unveiled</strong> the iPad in January 2010, Jobs held a “town hall”
	meeting with employees at Apple’s campus. Instead of <strong>exulting about</strong> their
	transformative new product, however, he <strong>went into a rant</strong> against Google for
	producing the rival Android operating system. Jobs was <strong>furious</strong> that Google had
	decided to compete with Apple in the phone business. “We did not enter the
	search business,” he said. “They entered the phone business. <strong>Make no mistake</strong>.
	They want to kill the iPhone. We won’t let them.” A few minutes later, after
	the meeting moved on to another topic, Jobs returned to his <strong>tirade</strong> to attack
	Google’s famous values slogan. “I want to go back to that other question first
	and say one more thing. This ‘Don’t be evil’ <strong>mantra</strong>, it’s <strong>bullshit</strong>.”
</p>

