</p>

<p>
	A few days after this rant, Jobs got a call from Schmidt, who had <strong>resigned</strong>
	from the Apple board the previous summer. He suggested they <strong>get together</strong> for
	coffee, and they met at a café in a Palo Alto shopping center. “We spent half
	the time talking about <strong>personal matters</strong>, then half the time on his <strong>perception</strong>
	that Google had stolen Apple’s user interface designs,” recalled Schmidt. When
	it came to the latter subject, Jobs did most of the talking. Google had ripped
	him off, he said in <strong>colorful language</strong>. “We’ve <strong>got you red-handed</strong>,” he told
	Schmidt. “I’m not interested in <strong>settling</strong>. I don’t want your money. If you
	offer me $5 billion, I won’t want it. I’ve got <strong>plenty of money</strong>. I want you to
	stop using our ideas in Android, that’s all I want.” They <strong>resolved nothing</strong>.
</p>

