	letter</strong>. Bill Campbell, his friend and board member, <strong>came by his house</strong> to <strong>go
	over it</strong>. “Does it sound like I’m just trying to <strong>stick it to Adobe</strong>?” he asked
	Campbell. “No, it’s facts, just <strong>put it out there</strong>,” the coach said. Most of the
	letter focused on the technical <strong>drawbacks</strong> of Flash. But despite Campbell’s
	coaching, Jobs <strong>couldn’t resist venting</strong> at the end about the problematic
	history between the two companies. “Adobe was the last major <strong>third party
	developer</strong> to fully adopt Mac OS X,” he noted.
</p>

