<p class="translation">
	Во многих компаниях потребительской электроники есть напряжение между
	дизайнерами, желающими сделать продукт красивым, и инженерами, которым нужно
	обеспечить соответствие функциональным требованиям. В Apple, где Джобс доводил
	до предела и дизайн, и инженерию, напряжение было ещё сильнее.
</p>

<p>
	When he and design director Jony Ive became creative <strong>coconspirators</strong> back in
	1997, they tended to view the <strong>qualms</strong> expressed by engineers as evidence of a
	<strong>can’t-do attitude</strong> that needed to be <strong>overcome</strong>. Their faith that awesome design
	could force <strong>superhuman feats</strong> of engineering was <strong>reinforced</strong> by the success of
	the iMac and iPod. When engineers said something couldn’t be done, Ive and
	Jobs pushed them to try, and usually they succeeded. There were occasional
	small problems. The iPod Nano, for example, <strong>was prone to getting scratched</strong>
	because Ive believed that a <strong>clear coating</strong> would <strong>lessen the purity</strong> of his
	design. But that was not a crisis.
</p>

