	К июню 2011 года планы четырёхэтажного здания площадью три миллиона
	квадратных футов, которое должно было вместить более двенадцати тысяч
	сотрудников, были готовы к показу. Он решил сделать это тихо и без
	афиширования — выступив перед городским советом Купертино на следующий
	день после анонса iCloud на Всемирной конференции разработчиков.
</p>

<p>
	Even though he had little energy, he had <strong>a full schedule</strong> that day. Ron
	Johnson, who had developed Apple’s stores and run them for more than a
	decade, had decided to <strong>accept an offer</strong> to be the CEO of J.C. Penney, and he
	came by Jobs’s house in the morning to discuss his <strong>departure</strong>. Then Jobs and
	I went into Palo Alto to a small yogurt and oatmeal café called Fraiche,
	where he <strong>talked animatedly</strong> about possible future Apple products. Later that
	day he was driven to Santa Clara for the <strong>quarterly meeting</strong> that Apple had
	with <strong>top Intel executives</strong>, where they discussed the possibility of using
	Intel chips in future mobile devices. That night U2 was playing at the
	Oakland Coliseum, and Jobs had <strong>considered going</strong>. Instead he decided to use
	that evening to show his plans to the Cupertino Council.
</p>

