</p>

<p>
	More significantly, in the early 2000s Jobs’s <strong>insistence on</strong> end-to-end
	integration gave Apple an advantage in developing a <strong>digital hub strategy</strong>,
	which allowed your desktop computer to <strong>link seamlessly</strong> with a variety of
	<strong>portable devices</strong>. The iPod, for example, was part of a closed and tightly
	integrated system. To use it, you had to use Apple’s iTunes software and
	<strong>download content</strong> from its iTunes Store. The result was that the iPod, like the
	iPhone and iPad that followed, was <strong>an elegant delight</strong> <strong>in contrast to</strong> the
	<strong>kludgy rival products</strong> that did not offer a seamless end-to-end experience.
