	master at <strong>putting together</strong> ideas, art, and technology in ways that <strong>invented
	the future</strong>. He designed the Mac after appreciating the power of <strong>graphical
	interfaces</strong> in a way that Xerox was unable to do, and he created the iPod after
	<strong>grasping the joy</strong> of having a thousand songs in your pocket in a way that Sony,
	which had all the <strong>assets and heritage</strong>, never could accomplish. Some leaders
	push innovations by being good at <strong>the big picture</strong>. Others do so by <strong>mastering
	details</strong>. Jobs did both, <strong>relentlessly</strong>. As a result he launched a series of
