<p>
	<strong>What drove me?</strong> I think most creative people want to <strong>express appreciation</strong> for
	being able to <strong>take advantage of</strong> the work that’s been done by others before us.
	I didn’t invent the language or mathematics I use. I make little of my own
	food, none of my own clothes. Everything I do depends on other members of our
	species and <strong>the shoulders that we stand on</strong>. And a lot of us want to <strong>contribute
	something back</strong> to our species and to <strong>add something to the flow</strong>. It’s about
	trying to express something in the only way that most of us know how—because
	we can’t write Bob Dylan songs or Tom Stoppard plays. We try to use the
	talents we do have to express our <strong>deep feelings</strong>, to show our appreciation of
	all the <strong>contributions</strong> that came before us, and to add something to that flow.
	That’s what has driven me.
</p>

